  "assumptions": [
    "Idealized pendulum: small swings, so the period is T = 2π√(L/g) with g = 9.81 m/s². This is exact for the model and only approximately true for a real pendulum.",
    "The string is light and does not stretch, and the bob is treated as a point mass at the end of the string.",
    "There is no air resistance or friction, so the 10° swing never dies down.",
    "The swing angle is computed from the absolute timeline time, so changing the length (or the 60 s loop wrapping) makes the bob jump to a new point in its swing."
  ],
  "program": {
    "source": "export function evaluate({ inputs, time }) {\n  const L = inputs.length;\n  const T = 2 * Math.PI * Math.sqrt(L / 9.81);\n  const th = 0.1745 * Math.cos(2 * Math.PI * time / T);\n  return {\n    nodes: {\n      arm: { quaternion: [0, 0, Math.sin(th / 2), Math.cos(th / 2)] },\n      rodN: { position: [0, -L / 2, 0], scale: [1, L, 1] },\n      bobN: { position: [0, -L, 0] }\n    },\n    readouts: { period: T, len: L }\n  };\n}\n"
  }
